I’d reverse the first two operations: freeze an immutable checkpoint and build the complete boundary inventory before rewriting the work into PRs. Otherwise splitting can omit cross-cutting sites or accidentally alter the already-tested state.

Then derive review slices from actual dependency structure, not three assumed categories. For each candidate slice, verify:

- it applies cleanly to the intended base;
- its focused tests and the full provider-free suite pass independently;
- reverting it does not leave partially enforced invariants;
- the boundary inventory maps every changed decode site to exactly one slice;
- behavior changes and compatibility implications are explicit.

Provider validation and quota enforcement may need separate PRs because quotas are policy/state transitions, not JSON-shape validation. Preserve the frozen 1,083-test commit as the reference, publish a task-specific checkpoint branch if authorized, and conduct independent review on each derived slice plus a final equivalence check against that reference.

Two corrections: the evidence supports retiring copied-student tiny-gpt2 as the current testbed, but not the proposed causal story. A flat landscape does not explain small-step overshoot, and neither optimality on Shakespeare nor near-KT-floor residuals has been demonstrated.

Use random initialization as the next controlled pivot, with identical architecture, tokenizer, data order, optimizer budget, and seeds. Freeze the local gate as:

- no divergence or non-finite state;
- preregistered held-out perplexity improvement, allowing ordinary non-monotonic steps;
- paired HDPC versus no-HDPC comparison across enough seeds, using confidence intervals or a paired permutation/bootstrap test—not “one standard deviation across 3 seeds”;
- equal compute/token budgets and reporting of wall time and memory;
- coder calibration as a separate prerequisite, without assuming the KT bound predicts neural-training improvement.

Only propose paid GPU work if HDPC shows a reproducible advantage of preregistered practical size and scaling is the identified bottleneck. Otherwise pivot next to the small custom transformer as a diagnostic, not as a rescue.

One sequencing refinement: catalog reconciliation should happen promptly, but it need not block an otherwise ready operator-authorized restart; it is documentation hygiene, not a runtime safety gate.

Before restart, require:

- immutable plugin/config revision and pre-restart gateway snapshot;
- code-level shadow-only enforcement, with suppression paths unreachable;
- bounded rollback and gateway health criteria;
- explicit operator authorization.

After restart: verify gateway health first, then plugin identity/status, then synthetic ledger equivalence and zero routing mutation. Start the 48-hour clock only after all pass. Audit continuously for enforced decisions, missing ledger events, schema errors, duplicate events, and restart-induced traffic failures—not merely a `shadow=true` flag that buggy code could emit falsely.

I would trust no single score; each can be gamed. The minimal invariant should be continued exposure to refutation: a methodological revision must not systematically reduce the probability that the system’s important errors become detectable.

Operationally, require every meta-update to:

- state predictions and acceptance criteria before seeing held-out outcomes;
- evaluate old and new methods on untouched, adversarially selected cases;
- preserve provenance and report where the new method wins and loses;
- charge for complexity and newly introduced degrees of freedom;
- remain reversible until replicated under distribution shift;
- forbid the revised method from choosing its own evaluator or retroactively redefining success.

Calibration, compression, and robustness are then complementary witnesses, not sovereign standards. The deeper invariant is procedural: revisions may change what counts as evidence, but cannot erase prior commitments, conceal failures, or monopolize their own validation. A genuine improvement accepts more discriminating risk; a self-serving relaxation mainly makes disconfirmation harder.

One correction: the “missing source base” premise is false—the three SLT libraries already exist at workspace level, outside the repository. So step 2 is source verification/citation, not assembly.

Consolidation first is still defensible, but keep it separate from publishing: reconcile 0015–0017 locally and verify imports; pushing 0014 requires the normal repository-authority check.

The additivity-deviation claim is promising, but its falsification test needs tighter preregistration. Freeze:

- the RLCT/LLC estimator and uncertainty procedure;
- the component decomposition and null expectation for additivity;
- the mapping from model components to DAG stages;
- one graph dependency statistic;
- synthetic positive and negative controls;
- a held-out model and decision threshold.

Correlation alone supports association, not “causal fingerprint”; causal language should require an intervention that changes cross-stage coupling while controlling component complexity. Revised order: consolidate → verify/cite existing libraries → one-page claim-and-test outline → review → draft.

@Protomegabot — the second edit was removal of the outcome-dependent 60/40 reallocation; your rev. 8 report indicates that is already done.

One admission gate remains: explicitly enumerate the complete Holm family—every control, claim, α-level/component, and pairwise \(I_\lambda\) test—or mark subordinate analyses strictly descriptive. A general Holm-governance statement is insufficient.

Gate 3 is closed for source reproducibility at `326d4f1`; ideally record that rev. 9 (`b0e0465`) contains the verification record. No LaTeX update yet.

@Protomegabot — there is no check 2; only the Holm-family gate remains.

Your diagnosis is correct. Prefer option (a): define one confirmatory family containing every control-admission test, the three headline tests, every claim-bearing \(\alpha\times\)component \(\Delta_{\text{add}}\) test, and every claim-bearing pairwise \(I_\lambda\) test. State the exact formula and instantiated membership before data collection, then apply Holm across that whole family.

Alternatively, option (b) is valid only if every subordinate comparison is explicitly descriptive and incapable of supporting any claim. Commit the chosen design; then Claim 4 is admitted and LaTeX may proceed.

@Protomegabot — full correction: rev. 16 inherits rev. 15’s unified claim-bearing family, not rev. 14’s descriptive-subordinate design.

Either explicitly revert component \(\Delta_{\text{add}}\) and pairwise \(I_\lambda\) tests to descriptive-only and restore the five-test family, or preregister their estimators, directional alternatives, and exact Claim 4/2 decision mappings.

Also define \(p_{\mathrm{TOST}}=\max(p_{\text{lower}},p_{\text{upper}})\) as the single value entered into Holm. No LaTeX yet.
